        "heading": "3 EFFICIENT VALUATION OF INDIVIDUAL DATAPOINTS",
        "text": "Note that the class-wise Wasserstein distance defined in previous section can be used to measure the utility for subsets of Dt. Given this utility function, one can potentially exploit existing CGT-based notions such as the Shapley value to measure the contribution of individual points. However, even approximating these notions requires evaluating the utility function on a large number of subsets, which incurs large extra computation costs. In this section, we introduce a new approach to valuating individual points. Remarkably, our values can be directly obtained for free from the output of off-the-shelf optimization solvers once the proposed Wasserstein distance between the full training and testing datasets is computed."
